---
description: Sort TS/JS members ascending; prioritize id fields
alwaysApply: true
---

# Sort ascending (TS/JS)

Always keep members in **ascending alphabetical** order.

**IDs first:** keys/fields named `id` or ending in `Id` (e.g. `productId`) come before other members; IDs are sorted among themselves A→Z, then the rest A→Z.

Applies to:

- Object literals and type/interface fields
- Union/enum members
- Named imports/exports and export statements
- Class members (when applicable)
- Consecutive property assignments (e.g. `this.code` before `this.name`)
- NestJS `@Module({ imports | providers | exports })` arrays:
  - **Static identifiers first** (A→Z), e.g. `FlashSaleModule`, `PrismaModule`
  - **Then dynamic `*.forRoot(...)` calls** (A→Z), e.g. `ConfigModule.forRoot`, `GraphQLModule.forRoot`
  - Provider object literals fall in a trailing group (after class providers)

Enforced by ESLint `eslint-plugin-perfectionist` in `@flash-sale/eslint-config`. Prefer `--fix` over hand-sorting when lint complains.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
type Props = { startsAt: Date; id: string; productId: string; endsAt: Date };

// ✅ GOOD — ids first, then A→Z
type Props = { id: string; productId: string; endsAt: Date; startsAt: Date };
```

```ts
// ❌ BAD — flat A→Z mixes forRoot with feature modules
@Module({
  imports: [
    ConfigModule.forRoot({ isGlobal: true }),
    FlashSaleModule,
    GraphQLModule.forRoot({ driver: ApolloDriver }),
    HealthModule,
  ],
})

// ✅ GOOD — static modules A→Z, then forRoot() A→Z
@Module({
  imports: [
    FlashSaleModule,
    HealthModule,
    ConfigModule.forRoot({ isGlobal: true }),
    GraphQLModule.forRoot({ driver: ApolloDriver }),
  ],
})
```
